(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["sbi-clerk"] = {"qs": [["Numerical Ability", "Find the next term of the series: 3, 6, 18, 72, 360, ?", ["1800", "2520", "2160", "1440"], 2, "Each term is multiplied by 2, 3, 4 and 5 in turn: 3×2=6, 6×3=18, 18×4=72, 72×5=360. The next multiplier is 6, so 360 × 6 = 2160."], ["Numerical Ability", "The present ages of two brothers are in the ratio 5 : 7. After 6 years the ratio will be 3 : 4. What is the present age of the elder brother?", ["42 years", "35 years", "28 years", "48 years"], 0, "Let ages be 5x and 7x. Then (5x + 6)/(7x + 6) = 3/4, so 20x + 24 = 21x + 18, giving x = 6. The elder brother is 7 × 6 = 42 years old."], ["English Language", "Which part of the sentence contains an error?\nShe don't know (A) / how to fill (B) / the deposit slip (C) / correctly. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 0, "The subject 'She' is third-person singular, so the correct form is 'She doesn't know', not 'She don't know'. The rest of the sentence is grammatically correct."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Which letter is 7th to the right of the 15th letter from the left end of the English alphabet?", ["V", "U", "W", "T"], 0, "The 15th letter from the left is O. Moving 7 places to the right means 15 + 7 = 22nd letter, which is V."], ["English Language", "Give one word for: 'A person who travels on foot along a road'.", ["Passenger", "Pilgrim", "Pedestrian", "Commuter"], 2, "A pedestrian is a person walking on a road. A passenger travels in a vehicle, a pilgrim travels to a holy place, and a commuter travels regularly between home and work, usually by vehicle."], ["English Language", "Fill in the blank with the correct verb form.\nNeither the manager nor the clerks ________ present at yesterday's meeting.", ["was", "is", "has been", "were"], 3, "With 'neither…nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject. The nearer subject 'clerks' is plural, and 'yesterday' needs the past tense, so 'were' is correct, not 'was'."], ["Numerical Ability", "Identify the number that does not fit the pattern: 1, 4, 9, 16, 26, 36", ["16", "26", "36", "9"], 1, "The series lists perfect squares: 1², 2², 3², 4², 5², 6². The fifth term should be 25, so 26 is the wrong number. All other terms are correct squares."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Statements: All pens are books. Some books are bags.\nConclusions: I. Some pens are bags. II. Some books are pens.", ["Only conclusion II follows", "Only conclusion I follows", "Both I and II follow", "Neither I nor II follows"], 0, "Since all pens are books, some books are definitely pens, so II follows. The bags that are books may lie outside the pen circle, so I is not certain."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Six persons J, K, L, M, N and O sit in a row facing north.\n• M sits fourth from the left end.\n• J sits second to the left of M.\n• N sits at one of the extreme ends.\n• K is an immediate neighbour of both M and N.\n• O sits immediately to the right of J.\n\nWho sits at the extreme left end?", ["N", "J", "L", "O"], 2, "M is 4th and J is 2nd. K must touch both M and N, so N is at the right end (6th) and K is 5th. O is 3rd, right of J, leaving L at the extreme left."], ["English Language", "Which part of the sentence contains an error?\nOne of my friend (A) / has opened (B) / a savings account (C) / in this bank. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 0, "'One of' is always followed by a plural noun, so it should be 'One of my friends'. The verb 'has opened' is correct because the subject 'One' is singular."], ["Reasoning Ability", "A is the brother of B. B is the daughter of C. D is the father of C. How is A related to D?", ["Son", "Grandson", "Nephew", "Granddaughter"], 1, "A and B are children of C, and D is C's father. So A is D's grandchild; since A is a brother, A is D's grandson. 'Granddaughter' would fit B, not A."], ["English Language", "Select the word that means the same as ABUNDANT.", ["Scarce", "Expensive", "Plentiful", "Hidden"], 2, "'Abundant' means available in large quantities, which matches 'plentiful'. 'Scarce' is its opposite, while 'expensive' and 'hidden' are unrelated to quantity."], ["English Language", "Improve the part in capitals, if needed.\nAfter signing the form, he RETURNED BACK it to the officer.", ["returned", "returned again", "had return back", "No improvement required"], 0, "'Return' already means to give or go back, so 'returned back' is redundant. The correct sentence is 'he returned it to the officer'. 'Had return back' is also ungrammatical."], ["Numerical Ability", "The average of five numbers is 40. When one number is removed, the average of the remaining four becomes 38. What number was removed?", ["42", "38", "50", "48"], 3, "Sum of five numbers = 5 × 40 = 200. Sum of four numbers = 4 × 38 = 152. The removed number = 200 − 152 = 48."], ["Numerical Ability", "A can complete a piece of work in 12 days and B in 24 days. In how many days can they finish it working together?", ["9 days", "8 days", "6 days", "18 days"], 1, "A does 1/12 and B does 1/24 of the work per day. Together: 1/12 + 1/24 = 3/24 = 1/8, so they finish in 8 days. Averaging 12 and 24 to get 18 is wrong."], ["Numerical Ability", "Find the value of ?\n48 ÷ 6 × 9 − 25 + 7² = ?", ["86", "96", "106", "94"], 1, "Following BODMAS: 7² = 49, then 48 ÷ 6 = 8 and 8 × 9 = 72. Finally 72 − 25 + 49 = 96. Division and multiplication are done left to right before addition and subtraction."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Complete the analogy: 6 : 35 :: 9 : ?", ["81", "80", "72", "90"], 1, "The rule is n² − 1: 6² − 1 = 35. Applying the same to 9 gives 81 − 1 = 80. The option 81 is only 9², missing the subtraction step."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Sunita walks 5 km towards the east, then turns left and walks 12 km. What is the shortest distance between her and the starting point?", ["13 km", "17 km", "12 km", "15 km"], 0, "Turning left from east means walking north. Her path forms a right angle, so the distance = √(5² + 12²) = √169 = 13 km. 17 km is the total distance walked, not the shortest."], ["English Language", "What does the idiom 'to break the ice' mean?", ["To start a conversation in an awkward situation", "To cause serious damage to something", "To end a long friendship suddenly", "To complete a difficult task quickly"], 0, "'To break the ice' means to say or do something that eases tension and starts a conversation, for example among strangers. It has nothing to do with damage, ending friendships or finishing tasks."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Which of these numbers is the odd one out: 27, 64, 144, 216?", ["27", "64", "144", "216"], 2, "27, 64 and 216 are perfect cubes (3³, 4³ and 6³). 144 is a perfect square (12²) but not a perfect cube, so it is the odd one out."], ["Reasoning Ability", "A man is facing north. He turns 90° clockwise and then 135° anticlockwise. Which direction is he facing now?", ["North-East", "South-West", "West", "North-West"], 3, "After turning 90° clockwise he faces east. Turning 135° anticlockwise from east takes him past north by 45°, so he faces north-west. The net turn is 45° anticlockwise from north."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question that follows.\nLakshmi runs a small tailoring shop in her village. For years she kept her earnings in a tin box at home. Last year a bank correspondent visited the village and helped her open a savings account using her Aadhaar card. Now she deposits money every week and has also started a small recurring deposit for her daughter's education. She says the account has given her both safety and discipline.\n\nWhy has Lakshmi started a recurring deposit?", ["To buy new machines for her tailoring shop", "To repay a loan taken from the bank", "To pay the bank correspondent's fee", "To save for her daughter's education"], 3, "The passage states that she started a small recurring deposit for her daughter's education. Shop machines, loan repayment and fees are not mentioned anywhere, so these options are distractors."], ["Numerical Ability", "Find the approximate value of ?\n24.98% of 639.9 + 15.02 × 6.97 = ?", ["265", "245", "285", "225"], 0, "Round off: 25% of 640 = 160 and 15 × 7 = 105. Adding gives 160 + 105 = 265. Rounding each value to the nearest convenient number keeps the error very small."], ["Numerical Ability", "What simple interest will ₹6,000 earn in 3 years at 8% per annum?", ["₹1,280", "₹1,560", "₹1,440", "₹1,500"], 2, "Simple interest = P × R × T / 100 = 6,000 × 8 × 3 / 100 = ₹1,440. Interest is calculated only on the original principal each year."], ["Numerical Ability", "Ravi's monthly salary of ₹25,000 is first increased by 10% and then the new salary is reduced by 10%. What is his final salary?", ["₹24,750", "₹25,000", "₹24,500", "₹25,250"], 0, "After the increase: 25,000 × 1.10 = 27,500. After the cut: 27,500 × 0.90 = 24,750. The 10% cut applies to a larger amount, so the salary does not return to ₹25,000."], ["Numerical Ability", "A 150 m long train is running at 54 km/h. How long will it take to pass an electric pole?", ["9 seconds", "12 seconds", "10 seconds", "15 seconds"], 2, "54 km/h × 5/18 = 15 m/s. To pass a pole, the train covers only its own length, so time = 150 / 15 = 10 seconds."], ["Numerical Ability", "Loans disbursed (₹ lakh) by four branches in a month: Branch A – 45, Branch B – 60, Branch C – 75, Branch D – 70.\nWhat percentage of the total loans was disbursed by Branch C?", ["28%", "32%", "25%", "30%"], 3, "Total disbursement = 45 + 60 + 75 + 70 = ₹250 lakh. Branch C's share = 75/250 × 100 = 30%. Check that the total is right before finding any percentage share."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Six persons J, K, L, M, N and O sit in a row facing north.\n• M sits fourth from the left end.\n• J sits second to the left of M.\n• N sits at one of the extreme ends.\n• K is an immediate neighbour of both M and N.\n• O sits immediately to the right of J.\n\nHow many persons sit between L and K?", ["Two", "Three", "Four", "One"], 1, "The final order from left is L, J, O, M, K, N. Between L (1st) and K (5th) sit J, O and M, which makes three persons."], ["English Language", "Select the word that is the opposite of ANCIENT.", ["Old", "Modern", "Historic", "Antique"], 1, "'Ancient' means belonging to the very distant past. Its opposite is 'modern'. 'Old', 'historic' and 'antique' all carry a sense of age, so they are similar in meaning, not opposite."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question that follows.\nLakshmi runs a small tailoring shop in her village. For years she kept her earnings in a tin box at home. Last year a bank correspondent visited the village and helped her open a savings account using her Aadhaar card. Now she deposits money every week and has also started a small recurring deposit for her daughter's education. She says the account has given her both safety and discipline.\n\nWhich word is closest in meaning to 'discipline' as used in the passage?", ["Punishment", "Freedom", "Regularity", "Confusion"], 2, "Here 'discipline' refers to the habit of saving money every week in a regular, controlled way. 'Punishment' is another meaning of the word but does not fit this context; 'freedom' and 'confusion' do not fit at all."], ["English Language", "Which of the following words is spelt correctly?", ["Comittee", "Commitee", "Comitee", "Committee"], 3, "The correct spelling has double 'm', double 't' and double 'e': C-O-M-M-I-T-T-E-E. Each wrong option drops one of these doubled letters, a frequent spelling error in exams."], ["Numerical Ability", "A shopkeeper buys a fan for ₹450 and sells it for ₹540. What is his profit percentage?", ["18%", "16.67%", "25%", "20%"], 3, "Profit = 540 − 450 = ₹90. Profit percentage is calculated on cost price: 90/450 × 100 = 20%. Calculating on selling price would wrongly give 16.67%."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question that follows.\nLakshmi runs a small tailoring shop in her village. For years she kept her earnings in a tin box at home. Last year a bank correspondent visited the village and helped her open a savings account using her Aadhaar card. Now she deposits money every week and has also started a small recurring deposit for her daughter's education. She says the account has given her both safety and discipline.\n\nWhere did Lakshmi keep her earnings before she opened the account?", ["With a local moneylender", "In a tin box at home", "In her daughter's school", "In a post office account"], 1, "The passage clearly says that for years she kept her earnings in a tin box at home. A moneylender, school or post office is never mentioned, so those options are unsupported."], ["English Language", "Which part of the sentence contains an error?\nThe cashier (A) / gave me (B) / a ten-rupees note (C) / as change. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 2, "When a number and a noun together act as an adjective before another noun, the noun stays singular: 'a ten-rupee note'. Hence 'ten-rupees' in part (C) is wrong."], ["Numerical Ability", "Find the compound interest on ₹10,000 for 1 year at 10% per annum, compounded half-yearly.", ["₹1,000", "₹1,025", "₹1,050", "₹1,100"], 1, "Half-yearly compounding means a rate of 5% for 2 periods. Amount = 10,000 × 1.05² = ₹11,025, so CI = ₹1,025. Annual compounding would give only ₹1,000."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Statements: No cup is a plate. All plates are bowls.\nConclusions: I. Some bowls are not cups. II. Some cups are bowls.", ["Only conclusion II follows", "Both I and II follow", "Neither I nor II follows", "Only conclusion I follows"], 3, "The bowls that are plates can never be cups, so some bowls are not cups and I follows. Cups may or may not overlap with bowls outside the plate region, so II is not definite."], ["Reasoning Ability", "If MONEY is written as NPOFZ in a code, how will CHEQUE be written?", ["DIERVF", "BGDPTD", "DIFRVF", "DIFRUF"], 2, "Each letter is replaced by the next letter of the alphabet: M→N, O→P and so on. Applying this to CHEQUE gives D, I, F, R, V, F, i.e. DIFRVF."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Among five friends, P is taller than Q. R is shorter than Q but taller than S. T is the tallest of all. Who is the shortest?", ["R", "Q", "P", "S"], 3, "The order from tallest is T, P, Q, R, S. Since R is shorter than Q but taller than S, and no one else is placed below S, S is the shortest."], ["Numerical Ability", "Find the next term of the series: 7, 10, 16, 25, 37, ?", ["49", "50", "54", "52"], 3, "The differences are 3, 6, 9 and 12, increasing by 3 each time. The next difference is 15, so the next term is 37 + 15 = 52."], ["English Language", "Fill in the blank with the correct word.\nMr. Sharma has been working at this branch ________ five years.", ["since", "for", "from", "by"], 1, "'For' is used with a period of time (five years), while 'since' is used with a point of time (since 2020). 'From' and 'by' do not fit with the present perfect continuous here."], ["Reasoning Ability", "If A = 1, B = 2, C = 3 and so on up to Z = 26, what is the total value of the word LOAN?", ["40", "42", "44", "38"], 1, "L = 12, O = 15, A = 1 and N = 14. Adding them: 12 + 15 + 1 + 14 = 42. Learning the positions of letters such as E, J, O, T and Y speeds up such questions."], ["English Language", "Arrange the sentences P, Q, R and S in a logical order.\nP: He filled in the withdrawal form carefully.\nQ: Amit went to the bank to withdraw some cash.\nR: Finally, the cashier handed him the money.\nS: He then submitted the form at the counter.", ["QSPR", "PQSR", "QPRS", "QPSR"], 3, "Q introduces Amit going to the bank, P describes filling the form, S ('then') shows him submitting it and R ('Finally') ends with receiving the cash. This is the only natural sequence."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Statements: M ≥ N > O; O ≥ P; Q > M\nConclusions: I. Q > O  II. N > P", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Both I and II follow", "Neither I nor II follows"], 2, "Combining: Q > M ≥ N > O ≥ P. So Q > O is definitely true, and N > O ≥ P gives N > P. Both conclusions follow."], ["Numerical Ability", "₹1,800 is divided among A, B and C in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4. How much does C receive?", ["₹800", "₹600", "₹720", "₹900"], 0, "Total parts = 2 + 3 + 4 = 9. C's share = 1,800 × 4/9 = ₹800. B would get ₹600 and A ₹400, which together with C's share total ₹1,800."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Rohit is 15th from the top and 26th from the bottom in his class. How many students are there in the class?", ["40", "41", "39", "42"], 0, "Total = position from top + position from bottom − 1 = 15 + 26 − 1 = 40. We subtract 1 because Rohit is counted in both positions."]]};
